{"duration_api_ms":28744,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"ca857fff-c98f-4ed4-af17-14fadb7ec8c3","total_cost_usd":0.1055838,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":4303,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4303,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4303,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":2403,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1055838,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters before being directed to Uranus.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\"},{\"text\":\"Mission planners directed Voyager 2 to Uranus.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\"},{\"text\":\"The journey of Voyager 2 to Uranus would take about 4,5 years.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2's encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\"},{\"text\":\"The geometry of Voyager 2's Uranus encounter was defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"The geometry of the Uranus encounter was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its Uranus flyby.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"The geometry of the Uranus encounter was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 was the first human-made object to fly past Uranus.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 began its short-range observations of Uranus on Nov. 4, 1985.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\"},{\"text\":\"On Nov. 4, 1985, signals from Voyager 2 took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\"},{\"text\":\"Light conditions at Uranus during the Voyager 2 encounter were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2's closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2's closest approach to Uranus was at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\"},{\"text\":\"During its Uranus flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The new Uranian moons discovered by Voyager 2 were given names such as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The names of the new Uranian moons are allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The tradition of naming Uranian moons after works of Shakespeare and Alexander Pope began in 1852.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"During its Uranus flyby, Voyager 2 discovered two new rings.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"Nine rings of Uranus were known before Voyager 2's flyby.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"During its Uranus flyby, Voyager 2 discovered a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus' magnetic field, discovered by Voyager 2, is off-center.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour).\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below the top cloud surface.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below Uranus' top cloud surface.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below the top cloud surface.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus' rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus' rings were found to be extremely variable in transparency.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 returned photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\"},{\"text\":\"Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania are five of Uranus' larger moons.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 flew by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers).\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"In flying by Miranda, Voyager 2 came closest to any object so far in its travels.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2's travels were nearly decade-long at the time of the Miranda flyby.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"Images of Miranda showed a surface that was a mishmash of peculiar features.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus appeared generally featureless.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\"},{\"text\":\"The news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the Challenger accident.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\"},{\"text\":\"The Challenger accident killed seven astronauts.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\"},{\"text\":\"The Challenger accident occurred during the space shuttle launch on Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\"}]}","ttft_ms":33259,"type":"result","duration_ms":33279,"uuid":"c97b87c1-0e05-4971-9809-03778a674fad","ttft_stream_ms":5747,"time_to_request_ms":4534,"first_content_frame_ms":5748,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
